import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Automated admission — the pipeline now emits the artifact, not just the verdict","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Automated admission — the pipeline now emits the artifact, not just the verdict","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-04T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/automated-admission.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/automated-admission.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/automated-admission.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="automated-admission-—-the-pipeline-now-emits-the-artifact-not-just-the-verdict" tabindex="-1">Automated admission — the pipeline now emits the artifact, not just the verdict <a class="header-anchor" href="#automated-admission-—-the-pipeline-now-emits-the-artifact-not-just-the-verdict" aria-label="Permalink to “Automated admission — the pipeline now emits the artifact, not just the verdict”">​</a></h1><p><code>law-proposer-extensions.md</code> ended the discovery loop at a ranked SHIP/no-ship report; <code>law-shape-aware.md</code> and <code>law-softmax-fold-shipped.md</code> then recorded the <em>last human step</em> — hand-writing the <code>R(...)</code>, its <code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> hooks, the test module, and the registration line into <code>catopt_core/laws/tensor.py</code>. That step is now emitted by the pipeline itself. Closing the loop is <code>pipeline --emit-admission &lt;candidate&gt;</code> → a reviewable patch, not hand-writing.</p><p>The new machinery is <code>tools/law_emit.py</code>, wired into <code>python -m catopt_discovery.pipeline</code> as <code>--emit-admission &lt;name&gt; --out &lt;dir&gt;</code>:</p><ul><li>the proposal&#39;s pattern trees render as <code>Op.make</code> calls with metavar / <code>$attr:</code>-metavar leaves (the same spelling <code>select_mul</code> / <code>softmax_fold</code> carry in <code>tensor.py</code>);</li><li><code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> hooks are transcribed <strong>verbatim</strong> from the proposer&#39;s named functions — <code>inspect.getsource</code> + a def-name round-trip check — never lambdas (emission refuses a hook with no honest source spelling);</li><li>a generated <code>tests/test_admitted_&lt;law&gt;.py</code> covers the match/instantiate round-trip, hook unit tests, firing + RHS-is-member, <strong>measured</strong> declines, and an end-to-end fire → cost-delta → cert-replay → <code>sink.verify</code> on the model the candidate fired on (the model term serialized into the test, feeds regenerated by name);</li><li><code>admission_&lt;law&gt;.patch</code> — a unified diff inserting the law block + a <code>SIMPLIFICATION_RULES</code> registration line into <code>tensor.py</code> plus the new test file — a <em>report for human review, not a mutation</em>. <code>admitted_&lt;law&gt;.py</code> is the same block as a standalone importable module so the artifact can be compiled and diffed against the library before anything is applied. Nothing under <code>packages/</code> was touched.</li></ul><p>Reproduce:</p><pre><code>.venv/bin/python python -m catopt_discovery.pipeline --holdout softmax_fold \\
    --emit-admission recognize:softmax --out /tmp/admission
</code></pre><p>CPU-only, ~6 min (the pipeline run dominates; emission adds seconds).</p><h2 id="_1-the-emitted-vs-handwritten-comparison-the-acceptance-test" tabindex="-1">1. The emitted-vs-handwritten comparison (the acceptance test) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-emitted-vs-handwritten-comparison-the-acceptance-test" aria-label="Permalink to “1. The emitted-vs-handwritten comparison (the acceptance test)”">​</a></h2><p>Both shipped machine-discovered laws are admitted, so the pipeline reports <code>recognize:softmax</code> as <code>duplicate</code> — nothing new to emit. The validation is therefore held out: <code>--holdout softmax_fold</code> makes <code>recognize:softmax</code> SHIP-able again, and it ranked <strong>#1 of 50, SHIP</strong> (identical evidence to the shipped run: <code>fires=1 paid=1 drop=19.0% cert=pass enode=1.06x</code>, held-out verdict PASS).</p><p>The emitted <code>RECOGNIZE_SOFTMAX</code> is <strong>functionally identical to the hand-written <code>SOFTMAX_FOLD</code></strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Same pattern</strong> — <code>law_proposal._key</code> alpha-normal keys are equal (same shape up to metavar naming: <code>U</code>/<code>RD</code>/<code>RK</code>/<code>SD</code> vs <code>u</code>/<code>RD</code>/<code>RK</code>/<code>SD</code>).</li><li><strong>Same hooks</strong> — the emitted <code>_check_sum_keepdim</code> / <code>_derive_softmax_dim</code> are verbatim transcriptions of the proposer&#39;s defs; <code>__code__.co_code</code> is <em>byte-identical</em> to the shipped hooks, and 0 diffs on a 21-binding battery (tuple/int/multi-axis/dimless dims × keepdim True/False/None).</li><li><strong>Same behaviour</strong> — on the real <code>ManualSoftmaxAttention</code> export: 1 firing each, identical extracted term (<code>softmax(div(matmul,…),dim=-1)</code>), identical cost <code>1.481e5</code>.</li><li><strong>Surface syntax differs</strong> — the rule name is <code>recognize_softmax</code> not <code>softmax_fold</code>, <code>law=</code> is a <code>TODO(reviewer)</code> placeholder, and the proposer&#39;s longer hook docstring rides along. Naming and prose are explicitly the review step; the math is not.</li></ul><p>Applied to a throwaway copy of <code>tensor.py</code> (the emitted patch, <code>git apply --check</code>-clean) and loaded as <code>catopt_core.laws.tensor</code> under a sandboxed import, the generated test module passes <strong>9/9</strong>: round-trip, check-accepts, derive-produces, fires + RHS-member, <code>RD</code>-extended decline, <code>RK</code>-flip decline, <code>RD</code>-scalar accepted variant, <code>U</code>-mismatch decline, and the e2e on the model export.</p><p>A second held-out class also emits and passes (<strong>7/7</strong>): the hook-free <code>census:mul_select</code> naturality law on <code>SelectiveSSM</code> — the shared-attr-metavar case. Its <em>accepted variants</em> are the instructive part: perturbing a <strong>shared</strong> <code>dim</code>/<code>index</code> binding (<code>dim+1</code>, <code>index+1</code>) still fires — measured, not assumed — while the declines are the attr-<em>mismatch</em> terms (second select&#39;s <code>dim</code>/<code>index</code> broken), structurally the same tests a human wrote for <code>select_mul</code>.</p><h2 id="_2-what-the-emitter-can-and-cannot-admit" tabindex="-1">2. What the emitter can and cannot admit <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-what-the-emitter-can-and-cannot-admit" aria-label="Permalink to “2. What the emitter can and cannot admit”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Emittable today:</strong> any SHIP candidate whose pattern trees use <code>Op</code>/<code>str</code>-metavar/<code>Var</code>/<code>Param</code>/<code>Const</code> leaves and literal attr values, with <code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> as named module-level functions (or absent). That covers every proposer family in the pool: census naturality, mixed-view, pattern recognition, shape-aware schemas, algebraic grammar.</p><p><strong>Refused honestly</strong> (<code>Emission.emitted=False</code> + reason, exit 1):</p><ul><li>non-SHIP candidates (the first failed gate is the reason, same as the report&#39;s &quot;why not&quot; column) and unknown names;</li><li>hooks with no source-level <code>def</code> — lambdas, <code>partial</code>s, closures, builtins. The proposer must carry the side condition as a named function for it to be transcribed;</li><li>terms carrying unspellable pieces — non-literal attr values (round-tripped through <code>ast.literal_eval(repr(v))</code>), non-kwarg attr keys, unknown-dim feeds (no honest <code>torch.randn</code>), an input name bound to two different types;</li><li>candidates needing a <strong>novel side condition the proposer did not carry</strong> — the deepest limit: the emitter transcribes what the proposal knows. A law that is true only under a shape guard nobody wrote is a proposer gap, not an emitter gap — it ships as an unconditional <code>R(...)</code> only if the pipeline&#39;s own gates (numeric oracle + <code>sink.verify</code>) already cleared it, and the review checklist says so.</li></ul><p><strong>Deliberately not automated:</strong> the <code>law=</code> prose, the name (the sanitized candidate label <code>recognize_softmax</code>, not <code>softmax_fold</code>), the tag set (<code>_SIM</code> default — an expansive law would belong in <code>CATEGORICAL_RULES</code>; the closure gate makes a SHIP-expansive unlikely, not impossible), and hook-name collisions (a <code>def</code> shadowing an existing <code>tensor.py</code> hook is flagged in the summary — in the holdout case the emitted defs are identical to the shipped ones and collapse).</p><h2 id="_3-emission-mechanics-worth-noting" tabindex="-1">3. Emission mechanics worth noting <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-emission-mechanics-worth-noting" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Emission mechanics worth noting”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Declines are probe-measured, not templated.</strong> The emitter perturbs every <code>$attr:</code> binding (bool flip, int +1, tuple extend / scalar-unwrap), runs <code>check</code> and a bare-e-graph firing on each, and emits a test only for the measured outcome — a <code>False</code> check or a non-firing term becomes a decline, a still-firing term becomes an <em>accepted variant</em> (the <code>dim=-1</code> int-spelling test the human wrote for <code>softmax_fold</code> falls out mechanically). Repeated metavars get structural mismatch terms: leaf metavars rebound to a fresh <code>Var</code>, attr metavars broken at their second position — the <code>u≠v</code> and <code>dim</code>/<code>index</code>-mismatch declines, exactly as hand-written.</li><li><strong>Serialization is lossless</strong> for the corpus: <code>Op.make</code> trees, <code>Var</code>/<code>Param</code>/<code>Const</code> leaves, <code>repr()</code>-round-tripped attrs; the emitted <code>_MODEL_TERM</code> compares <code>op_repr</code>-equal to the real export. Inputs are keyed by <em>name</em> — a <code>Var</code> leaf occurring N times is one input (the SelectiveSSM export carries six <code>x</code> leaves; emitting six feeds silently unbound <code>x</code>, caught as a <code>sink.verify</code> failure and fixed).</li><li><strong>Hooks transcribe verbatim.</strong> The first implementation renamed hooks to <code>_check_&lt;law&gt;</code> and hit a transcription failure in the real <code>__main__</code> run that never reproduced in tests; verbatim emission under the proposer&#39;s own names is both more faithful (they mirror the shipped shapes) and removes the fragile step. A def-name sanity check + in-memory <code>ruff format</code> + <code>compile()</code> on every artifact guards what remains.</li></ul><h2 id="_4-gates" tabindex="-1">4. Gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-gates" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Gates”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>.venv/bin/ruff check tools</code> — pass</li><li><code>.venv/bin/ruff format --check tools</code> — pass</li><li><code>.venv/bin/ty check</code> — pass (tools are outside <code>[tool.ty.src]</code>; <code>packages/</code> untouched)</li><li><code>.venv/bin/vulture</code> — pass</li><li><code>.venv/bin/lint-imports</code> — 4 contracts kept</li><li><code>.venv/bin/bandit -c .bandit.yaml -r packages</code> — 0 findings</li><li><code>.venv/bin/semgrep --config .semgrep.yml packages</code> — 0 findings</li><li><code>scripts/radon_ratchet.py</code> — pass</li><li><code>law_pipeline.py --holdout softmax_fold</code> — <strong>PASS</strong> (<code>recognize:softmax</code> rank 1 of 50, SHIP; emitted artifact applied to a throwaway <code>tensor.py</code> → generated tests 9/9 pass under a sandboxed import; <code>git apply --check</code> clean)</li><li>Emitted-vs-handwritten: <code>_key</code> equality, hook bytecode identity, 0/21 binding diffs, identical firing/extraction on the real model</li></ul><p>The suite itself was not re-run (tools-only change; <code>packages/</code> and <code>tests/</code> untouched — the emitted test file is a report artifact in <code>/tmp</code>, not an in-tree addition).</p><h2 id="_5-where-this-leaves-the-loop" tabindex="-1">5. Where this leaves the loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-where-this-leaves-the-loop" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Where this leaves the loop”">​</a></h2><p>Discovery → verdict → artifact is now one command; the human reviews a diff instead of writing code. The remaining manual surface is deliberately small: the <code>law=</code> sentence, the rule name, the tag set. The next extension is on the <em>proposer</em> side — a recognizer for the un-recognized chains (<code>(mul, add, rsqrt)</code> RMSNorm, SwiGLU/GEGLU — <code>law-proposer-extensions.md</code> §4), where the proposer must also carry the <code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> the emitter will then transcribe verbatim.</p>`,25)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};